1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,084
Host: Welcome to KC Desk Notes. Today we are unpacking one fresh research report and the question it raises.

2
00:00:06,444 --> 00:00:12,574
Host: Workday just reported its fiscal first-quarter results, and the stock popped 11 percent after hours.

3
00:00:12,934 --> 00:00:21,757
Guest: That’s right. It was a beat on sUBScription revenue, margins, and EPS, and it came after a tough fourth quarter and fairly bearish expectations.

4
00:00:22,117 --> 00:00:24,811
Host: So what was the main surprise that changed the narrative?

5
00:00:25,171 --> 00:00:30,326
Guest: The headline is that Workday delivered its best first quarter for new net annual contract value in five years.

6
00:00:30,686 --> 00:00:33,890
Guest: And they gave more concrete details on how AI can drive revenue.

7
00:00:34,250 --> 00:00:40,009
Host: Concrete details—that’s what investors have been waiting for. What did they actually lay out?

8
00:00:40,369 --> 00:00:49,889
Guest: They outlined three clear monetization paths for AI. First, selling their own AI agents and taking full responsibility for total cost of ownership. Second,

9
00:00:50,249 --> 00:00:55,218
Guest: growing their Extend Pro platform so customers can build their own AI applications on top of Workday.

10
00:00:55,578 --> 00:00:56,878
Host: And the third path?

11
00:00:57,238 --> 00:01:04,901
Guest: Providing the APIs and infrastructure for an ecosystem of third-party developers. So it’s not just one AI product—it’s a whole platform strategy.

12
00:01:05,261 --> 00:01:09,208
Host: That sounds ambitious. But does the core business still have momentum?

13
00:01:09,568 --> 00:01:14,351
Guest: Yes, but it’s stable rather than accelerating. If you strip out AI-related revenue,

14
00:01:14,711 --> 00:01:21,538
Guest: core sUBScription growth has been around 12 percent for five straight quarters. The AI layer is what could reaccelerate growth.

15
00:01:21,898 --> 00:01:25,753
Host: And they also announced entry into IT service management. Why does that matter?

16
00:01:26,113 --> 00:01:33,682
Guest: It expands their total addressable market beyond HR and finance into the employee lifecycle more broadly. It’s a logical extension of their domain expertise.

17
00:01:34,042 --> 00:01:41,055
Host: So the beat was solid, and the AI narrative is getting more tangible. But the report still keeps a Neutral rating. Why?

18
00:01:41,415 --> 00:01:47,823
Guest: Because a sustained re-rating depends on successful rollout of organically built AI agents and product-market fit for these newer offerings.

19
00:01:48,183 --> 00:01:51,016
Guest: The next twelve months are really a transition year.

20
00:01:51,376 --> 00:01:53,977
Host: So what’s the one thing the report doesn’t fully settle?

21
00:01:54,337 --> 00:02:00,606
Guest: Whether the “headless” enterprise software vision actually drives material revenue growth, or if it’s still an experiment.

22
00:02:00,966 --> 00:02:04,542
Guest: The full report goes deeper into that risk and the valuation framework.

23
00:02:04,902 --> 00:02:07,456
Host: Good point. We’ll leave that for the community discussion.

24
00:02:07,816 --> 00:02:15,479
Guest: Exactly. There’s a lot more in the full note on the GAAP-based valuation change and the margin trajectory. Worth a read if you’re following the software space.
